{
  "id": "tokio_rs_tokio_8408",
  "repo_name": "tokio-rs/tokio",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio",
  "base_commit": "0d38ab548057244ecbe710acc0c264b26b15dc95",
  "pr_number": 8408,
  "issue_number": 8406,
  "issue_title": "spawn_blocking task is silently orphaned when the blocking pool contains only scheduler workers and thread spawn returns EAGAIN",
  "issue_body": "**Version**\n\n```\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 tokio v1.53.1\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 tokio-macros v2.7.2 (proc-macro)\n```\n\nReproduced on 1.53.1 (current release) and the code path is unchanged on\n`master` as of this writing.\n\n**Platform**\n\n```\nLinux 6.8.0-138-generic #138-Ubuntu SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC x86_64 GNU/Linux\nrustc 1.94.1\n```\n\nOriginally hit in production on Linux 6.12 x86_64 / glibc.\n\n## Description\n\nOn a multi-threaded runtime, `spawn_blocking` can hang forever with no error\nand no thread doing any work. It happens when the blocking pool's thread count\nconsists solely of scheduler workers and the OS refuses one more thread with\n`EAGAIN`.\n\n[`blocking/pool.rs`][pool] pushes the task, fails to spawn a thread, and\nswallows the error:\n\n```rust\nErr(ref e)\n    if is_temporary_os_thread_error(e)\n        && self.inner.metrics.num_threads() > 0 =>\n{\n    // OS temporarily failed to spawn a new thread.\n    // The task will be picked up eventually by a currently\n    // busy thread.\n}\n```\n\nThe assumption is that some currently busy pool thread will return to the queue\nloop. That does not hold at this moment, because the only threads counted are\nthe runtime's own workers: [`multi_thread/worker.rs:489`][worker] (and\n[`:514`][worker2]) launch each worker via\n`runtime::spawn_blocking(move || run(worker))`, and\n[`builder.rs:2014`][builder] sizes the pool as\n`max_blocking_threads + worker_threads` to account for them. A worker sits\ninside `run(worker)` for the runtime's whole life and never returns to\n`Inner::run`'s `while let Some(task) = shared.queue.pop_front()`. So the task\nhas no possible executor.\n\nConsequence: the caller awaits a `JoinHandle` that can never resolve, every\nworker parks, and the process burns zero CPU. Externally it is\nindistinguishable from slow work.\n\n### Where the assumption comes from\n\nThis branch was introduced in #4485 to fix #2309 (the runtime used to panic\nwhen the OS refused a thread). Its stated rationale is:\n\n> Ignore the temporary errors when failing to spawn a new thread from the OS.\n> The task will be naturally scheduled by threads already in the pool.\n\nThat is sound whenever real pool threads exist. It is not sound for the *first*\n`spawn_blocking` of a process, where `num_threads() > 0` is satisfied entirely\nby scheduler workers that never drain the pool queue. In that window #4485\nconverts a loud panic into a silent permanent hang.\n\nThe sibling branch a few lines up rests on the same assumption:\n\n```rust\nif self.inner.metrics.num_threads() == self.inner.thread_cap {\n    // At max number of threads\n}\n```\n\nThat one is also sound only while real pool threads exist to drain the queue.\n\n## Reproducer\n\nDeterministic; hangs on every run. It calls `setrlimit` on **its own process**\nonly, so it does not affect the system.\n\n`Cargo.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[dependencies]\ntokio = { version = \"1.53.1\", features = [\"full\"] }\nlibc = \"0.2\"\n```\n\n```rust\nuse std::time::Duration;\n\nfn set_nproc_limit(soft: u64) {\n    let lim = libc::rlimit { rlim_cur: soft, rlim_max: soft };\n    assert_eq!(unsafe { libc::setrlimit(libc::RLIMIT_NPROC, &lim) }, 0);\n}\nfn threads_now() -> u64 {\n    std::fs::read_dir(\"/proc/self/task\").map(|d| d.count() as u64).unwrap_or(1)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()\n        .worker_threads(4)\n        .enable_all()\n        .build()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    rt.block_on(async {\n        tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(300)).await; // workers up\n\n        // Control arm: pre-warm ONE real pool thread before the limit applies.\n        if std::env::var(\"WARM\").is_ok() {\n            let _ = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(|| ()).await;\n        }\n\n        set_nproc_limit(threads_now());                       // no more threads\n\n        // Prove thread creation really fails now.\n        match std::thread::Builder::new().spawn(|| {}) {\n            Ok(h) => { let _ = h.join(); println!(\"limit too loose\"); }\n            Err(e) => println!(\"plain spawn fails: {e} ({:?})\", e.kind()), // WouldBlock\n        }\n\n        // First spawn_blocking of the process: the pool holds only workers.\n        match tokio::time::timeout(\n            Duration::from_secs(5),\n            tokio::task::spawn_blocking(|| 42u32),\n        ).await {\n            Ok(Ok(v)) => println!(\"returned {v}\"),\n            Ok(Err(e)) => println!(\"failed cleanly: {e}\"),\n            Err(_) => println!(\"*** HUNG *** task orphaned, no error\"),\n        }\n    });\n}\n```\n\nI expected to see this happen: the task runs, or the `JoinHandle` resolves to\nan error.\n\nInstead, this happened:\n\n```\n$ cargo run --release\nplain spawn fails: Resource temporarily unavailable (os error 11) (WouldBlock)\n*** HUNG *** task orphaned, no error\n```\n\nThe only difference in the control arm is that one real pool thread exists\nbefore thread creation is denied:\n\n```\n$ WARM=1 cargo run --release\nplain spawn fails: Resource temporarily unavailable (os error 11) (WouldBlock)\nreturned 42            # in ~19 \u00b5s\n```\n\nEverything else \u2014 same binary, same `RLIMIT_NPROC` mechanism, same denial of\nthread creation \u2014 is identical. That control is what identifies the pool's\ncomposition, rather than the thread-creation failure itself, as the deciding\nfactor.\n\n## Expected behaviour\n\n`spawn_blocking` should either run the task or fail it. If the only counted\nthreads are scheduler workers, \"a currently busy thread will pick it up\" is\nfalse and the task should be rejected the way `SpawnError::NoThreads` already\nis (the `JoinHandle` resolves to a cancelled `JoinError`).\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n1. Track pool threads separately from scheduler workers, and take the\n   swallow-the-error branch only when `num_pool_threads > 0`. The same count\n   should gate the `num_threads == thread_cap` branch.\n2. Failing that, retry the spawn on the next `spawn_blocking`, or park a timer\n   to retry, so a queued task is not left with no possible executor.\n\n## Field report\n\nReal occurrence: a Lance compaction whose first local-file read is a\n`spawn_blocking` \u2014 the first one of the process. Twice it stopped dead before\nthat read, with all 25 threads parked, no blocking thread at all, zero CPU and\nzero IO for hours. Process and thread limits were never close (peak 2,013 of\n83,191), so the `EAGAIN` was transient rather than a configuration problem. I\nhave thread-apply-all-backtrace output from both incidents and can attach it if\nuseful.\n\nTwo workarounds, both effective: build the runtime explicitly and warm one\nblocking thread before any real work, and raise `thread_keep_alive` so the warm\nthread stays.\n\n[pool]: https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/tokio-1.53.1/tokio/src/runtime/blocking/pool.rs#L427-L434\n[worker]: https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/tokio-1.53.1/tokio/src/runtime/scheduler/multi_thread/worker.rs#L489\n[worker2]: https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/tokio-1.53.1/tokio/src/runtime/scheduler/multi_thread/worker.rs#L514\n[builder]: https://github.com/tokio-rs/tokio/blob/tokio-1.53.1/tokio/src/runtime/builder.rs#L2014\n",
